INT. ABANDONED OBSERVATORY, MAIN DOME - NIGHT

A great copper dome, its slit open to the stars, hangs overhead like the underside of a bell. A brass telescope as long as a bus rests on a cracked iron mount. Dead leaves cover the floor. A single lantern burns on a crate.

CASPIAN ROTH, fifties, in a long gray coat, sits on an overturned bucket with a laptop open on his knees. He types, stops, types again. His face glows pale blue. Beside him, a satellite phone lies on the crate, its screen dark.

He lifts his head. The dome creaks in a wind that shouldn't be there.

                    CASPIAN
          (to himself)
          Come on. Come on.

He types. The telescope's great eye twitches an inch to the left, and a gear somewhere shrieks. He freezes. The dome is still.

                    CASPIAN (CONT'D)
          That's not me.

He closes the laptop slowly and reaches for the phone. As he does, a shape detaches itself from the shadow beneath the telescope and walks forward into the light: ODETTE LACROIX, forties, in a black raincoat, bare hands, no bag. She is soaked to the skin. A thin line of water drips from her hem onto the dead leaves with every step.

                    ODETTE
          Don't touch the phone.

                    CASPIAN
          How long have you been here?

                    ODETTE
          Since before you.

                    CASPIAN
          That's impossible. I locked the door.

                    ODETTE
          Which door?

He looks at the entrance, a heavy oak slab bolted from inside. Then at the slit overhead. Then at her wet coat.

                    CASPIAN
          Who sent you?

                    ODETTE
          Nobody sends me. I'm the one who stays.

She crosses to the crate and looks down at the dead phone, then at the laptop, then at him. Her eyes are tired, not unkind.

                    ODETTE (CONT'D)
          You came here to upload something.

                    CASPIAN
          Data. Just data.

                    ODETTE
          Data about the star?

                    CASPIAN
          How do you

                    ODETTE
          I'm the last person who could read it.

A long silence. A gust of cold air sweeps through the dome. The lantern gutters, steadies.

                    CASPIAN
          (low, to the room)
          Is anyone else here?

                    ODETTE
          Only the telescope.

                    CASPIAN
          It moved.

                    ODETTE
          It does that when someone looks.

He sets the laptop on the crate and stands. She doesn't step back. Their shadows overlap on the leaf-strewn floor.

                    CASPIAN
          Miss, I don't know what

                    ODETTE
          Caspian. Sit down. You'll want to hear this sitting.

He stays standing. Then, slowly, as if all his joints have turned to paper, he lowers himself back onto the bucket. She picks up the lantern and holds it between them, so that each of them is lit and neither is.

                    ODETTE (CONT'D)
          Tell me what you saw.
